#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

== Equivalences of subgroup complexes <sec:subgroup-complexes>

#subhead[Quillen's equivalence $cal(A)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$]#idx(
  "equivalence",
)#idx(
  "equivalence",
  "Quillen's, $cal(A)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$.",
)#idx("Quillen", "'s equivalence $cal(A)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$.") Recall
that we denote by $cal(S)_p (G)$ the poset of non-trivial $p$-subgroups of $G$,
and by $cal(A)_p (G)$ the poset of non-trivial elementary abelian#idx(
  "elementary",
  "abelian group",
)#idx("group", "elementary abelian") $p$-subgroups of $G$.

#theorem(title: [Quillen, Thévenaz])[
  The inclusion of $cal(A)_p (G)$ in $cal(S)_p (G)$ is a $G$-homotopy
  equivalence.
] <th:quillen-equivalence>

#proof[
  Let $i : cal(A)_p (G) arrow.r.hook cal(S)_p (G)$ be the inclusion. By
  Theorem~@th:g-homotopy-equivalence and Quillen's Lemma~@lem:quillen it
  suffices to show that for each subgroup $H <= G$ and for each non-trivial
  $p$-subgroup $P <= G$ normalised by $H$, the poset
  $(i slash P)^H = cal(A)_p (P)^H$ is contractible.

  Denote by $P_0 = Omega_1 Z(P)$ the subgroup of the centre of $P$ generated by
  elements of order $p$, so that $P_0$ is a non-trivial central elementary
  abelian #source(237)$p$-subgroup of $P$, also normalised by $H$. Then for any
  non-trivial elementary abelian subgroup $A <= P$ normalised by $H$, we have
  $ A <= chevron.l A, P_0 chevron.r >= P_0. $
  Since $chevron.l A, P_0 chevron.r$ is elementary abelian, this means that
  $cal(A)_p (P)^H$ is conically contractible.
]

#remark(kind: "Comment")[
  The homotopy equivalence $cal(A)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$ is due to
  Quillen [@bib:Quillen1978]. Thévenaz [@bib:Thevenaz1987] showed that the
  homotopy equivalence is in fact $G$-equivariant.

  Quillen conjectured that $cal(S)_p (G)$ is contractible if and only if
  $O_p (G) != 1$. The "if" is clear since if $O_p (G) != 1$, then $cal(S)_p (G)$
  is conically contractible via
  $ P <= P O_p (G) >= O_p (G). $
  He also proved his conjecture in the case where $G$ is solvable. It should be
  remarked that if $cal(S)_p (G)$ is $G$-contractible then $O_p (G) != 1$, since
  in particular $G$ has to have a fixed point on $cal(S)_p (G)$.
]

#subhead[Bouc's equivalence $cal(B)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$]#idx(
  "Bouc's equivalence $cal(B)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$",
)#idx("equivalence", "Bouc's, $cal(B)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$") Recall that
we denote by $cal(B)_p (G)$ the poset of non-trivial $p$-subgroups which are
equal to the $O_p$ of their normaliser.

Bouc's observation is that if $P in cal(S)_p (G)^H$, $P in.not cal(B)_p (G)^H$,
then
$ {Q in cal(S)_p (G)^H | Q > P} $
is contractible, since
$ Q >= N_Q (P) <= N_Q (P) O_p N_G (P) >= O_p N_G (P). $
To make use of this, we introduce the following notation:

#definition[
  If $X$ is a poset and $x, y in X$, we set
  $
    [x, y]_X & = {z in X | x <= z <= y} \
    (x, y)_X & = {z in X | x < z < y}
  $
  etc. In particular,
  $
    [x, -)_X & = {z in X | x <= z} \
    (x, -)_X & = {z in X | x < z}
  $
  If $B subset.eq X$, we set
  $
    e_X (B) & = {x in X | (-, x]_X inter B upright("is contractible")} \
    f_X (B) & = {x in X | [x, -)_X inter B upright("is contractible")}
  $
] <def:poset-intervals>

#lemma[
  If $B subset.eq Y subset.eq e_X (B)$ (resp. $B subset.eq Y subset.eq f_X (B)$)
  then these inclusions are homotopy equivalences. In particular, if
  $e_X (B) = X$ then $Y tilde.eq X$.
] <lem:contractible-intervals>

#proof[
  Apply Quillen's Lemma~@lem:quillen to the inclusions $B arrow.r.hook Y$ and
  $B arrow.r.hook e_X (B)$.
]

#definition[
  #source(238)Let
  $
    X^* & = {x in X | (x, -)_X italic("is not contractible")} \
    X_* & = {x in X | (-, x)_X italic("is not contractible")}
  $

  A poset $X$ has *bounded height*#idx("bounded height")#idx("height, bounded")
  if there is an upper bound for the length of a chain
  $ x_0 < x_1 < dots.c < x_n. $
  This is the same as saying that the simplicial realisation of $X$ is a finite
  dimensional complex.
] <def:bounded-height>

#proposition(title: [Bouc])[
  Suppose that $X$ has bounded height. If $B$ is a subset of $X$ containing
  $X^*$ (resp. containing $X_*$) then the inclusions
  $X^* subset.eq B subset.eq X$ (resp. $X_* subset.eq B subset.eq X$) are
  homotopy equivalences.
] <prop:bouc-bounded-height>

#proof[
  It suffices to treat the inclusion $X^* subset.eq B$, since $X^* subset.eq X$
  is a special case of this. Denote by $i$ the inclusion $X^* arrow.r.hook B$.
  We claim that for $b in B$, the poset
  $b class("normal", backslash) i = [b, -)_(X^*)$ is contractible, whereupon the
  proposition follows by applying Quillen's Lemma~@lem:quillen.

  We prove this claim by induction on the height of $X$. If $b in X^*$, the
  claim is clear, since $[b, -)_(X^*)$ has a unique minimal element, and is
  therefore conically contractible. Otherwise, we have
  $[b, -)_(X^*) = (b, -)_(X^*)$. What we know from the definition of $X^*$ is
  that $(b, -)_X$ is contractible, so we need to show that the inclusion
  $(b, -)_(X^*) arrow.r.hook (b, -)_X$ is a homotopy equivalence. But this is a
  case of the proposition of strictly smaller height, and therefore the
  proposition is proved by induction.
]

We can now apply this proposition together with the observation made earlier to
deduce Bouc's theorem.

#theorem(title: [Bouc, Thévenaz])[
  The inclusion of $cal(B)_p (G)$ in $cal(S)_p (G)$ is a $G$-homotopy
  equivalence.
] <th:bouc-equivalence>

#proof[
  This follows by applying Theorem~@th:g-homotopy-equivalence and the above
  proposition, since we have $cal(B)_p (G)^H supset.eq (cal(S)_p (G)^H)^*$.
]

#remark(kind: "Comment")[
  The statement that $cal(B)_p (G) tilde.eq cal(S)_p (G)$ is due to Bouc
  [@bib:Bouc1984b], and Thévenaz showed that the homotopy equivalence is in fact
  a $G$-homotopy equivalence.
]

#exercise[
  Let $cal(Z)_p (G)$#idx("$cal(Z)_p (G)$") be the poset of non-trivial
  elementary abelian $p$-subgroups $A <= G$ such that
  $A = Omega_1 O_p Z C_G (A)$. Prove that the inclusion is a homotopy
  equivalence $cal(Z)_p (G) tilde.eq_G cal(S)_p (G)$.
]
